            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-accent">XVII · Zikr Symphony</p>
              <h2 class="mb-8 text-4xl font-heading font-bold leading-tight text-maintext md:text-5xl">
                The first major concept album
              </h2>

              <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-8 text-maintext/80">
                <p>
                  The idea of a “zikr symphony” had existed in Köksal's mind for a
                  long time. He wanted to move zikr beyond a narrow inherited musical
                  form and place the remembrance of the ONE GOD inside the full sound
                  world of contemporary production: orchestra, choir, bass, drums,
                  electric guitar and modern recording technology.
                </p>

                <p>
                  The project was designed around the number 19 in reference to the
                  QUR'AN's 19-based system as understood by Köksal. During production,
                  he created roughly fifty underlying musical sketches. As the album
                  took shape, the material was reduced and organized into 19 main
                  works.
                </p>

                <p>
                  The nineteenth work became a second level of the concept: rather
                  than discarding musical ideas he valued, Köksal brought them
                  together into a further 19-part medley. The number 19 therefore
                  operates not merely as a track count but as part of the album's
                  architecture.
                </p>

                <p>
                  The cover's male and female-looking figures were intended as an
                  artistic metaphor, not as a literal statement about the gender of
                  angels. Köksal describes the image as a symbolic celebration in
                  which creation joins in glorifying GOD; even the rock hand gesture
                  belongs to that visual metaphor.
                </p>

                <p>
                  Zikr Symphony was also one of the last major albums on which Köksal
                  placed his own bass-baritone voice prominently at the front. In
                  later work he increasingly chose voices according to the needs of
                  the composition rather than the desire to maintain a personal lead
                  vocalist identity.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- XVIII -->
      <section>
        <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-accent">XVIII · The Expanding Album Universe</p>
        <h2 class="mb-12 text-4xl font-heading font-bold leading-tight text-maintext md:text-5xl">
          One spiritual center, many musical languages
        </h2>

