      <!-- XIX -->
      <section class="pro-glass p-8 md:p-14">
        <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-accent">XIX · Why the Albums Sound So Different</p>
        <h2 class="mb-8 text-4xl font-heading font-bold leading-tight text-maintext md:text-5xl">
          Contrast became part of the artistic philosophy
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-8 text-subtext">
          <p>
            The movement from <em>Zikr Symphony</em> to <em>Zikr Huu</em> and
            <em>Zikr Nur</em> was deliberately contrasting. After the scale and density
            of symphonic writing, Köksal wanted to explore a nearly weightless form of
            remembrance: female voices, minimal instrumentation, meditation, rain,
            ney and quiet atmosphere.
          </p>

          <p>
            <em>ZIKR ROCK</em> then moved to the opposite pole, reconnecting the
            sacred project with the rock and guitar language that had shaped him from
            youth. The album begins with <em>Huu</em>, followed by
            <em>Allahu Ekber</em> and <em>Ya Hannan Ya Mennan Zül Celali vel İkram</em>,
            and grows progressively more distorted and energetic toward its final
            <em>Subhanallah</em>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Köksal personally rates the album's mix and mastering at roughly eight
            out of ten and regards the work as one of the strongest expressions of
            his rock identity within the sacred catalogue.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- XX -->
      <section class="pro-glass p-8 md:p-14">
        <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-accent">XX · Classical Chorus and the Name Neo-Sacred Zikr</p>
        <h2 class="mb-8 text-4xl font-heading font-bold leading-tight text-maintext md:text-5xl">
          The music existed before the umbrella name
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-8 text-subtext">
          <p>
            The earliest zikr singles and albums were not originally created under a
            fully formalized “Neo-Sacred Zikr” label. The name emerged later, around
            the <strong class="text-maintext">Classical Chorus</strong> period and the
            development of Köksal's website.
          </p>

          <p>
            By that time, the catalogue already included radically different musical
            directions. Köksal needed a higher-level concept capable of describing
            what connected them. The answer was not a conventional genre definition,
            but a musical universe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Classical Chorus brought Köksal back toward the sacred choral language he
            had loved for years. Bach remained an important structural reference in
            his broader development, while Mozart's dramatic and emotional influence
            — particularly the world of Requiem and Kyrie-like choral writing — was
            closer to the spirit he wanted to express here.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- XXI -->
      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <article class="pro-card p-7 md:p-9">
          <p class="mb-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-accent">One God One Love</p>
          <h2 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-maintext">Children also need a language of remembrance</h2>
          <div class="space-y-5 leading-8 text-subtext">
            <p>
              Arya Belinda's early interest in zikr helped inspire the idea of a
              children's choir album. Köksal felt that monotheist children should also
              have contemporary songs through which they could remember and glorify
              GOD.
            </p>
            <p>
              The album uses a 19-track structure and includes English language as
              part of its more universal orientation. Arya Belinda is also represented
              among the children on the cover concept.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="pro-card p-7 md:p-9">
          <p class="mb-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-accent">ZIKR RAP</p>
          <h2 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-maintext">Turning a disliked form into a new possibility</h2>
          <div class="space-y-5 leading-8 text-subtext">
            <p>
              Rap had not originally been one of Köksal's favorite musical forms. He
              associated much of what he heard in the genre with repetitive backing
              tracks, profanity and negative language.
            </p>
            <p>
              That resistance eventually became the reason to make the album. If rap
              could carry so many other messages, why could it not carry remembrance
              of GOD? The result was intentionally contrary to expectation, beginning
              with Bismillahirrahmanirrahim and redirecting rap's rhythmic force
              toward sacred content.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- XXII -->
      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <article class="pro-card p-7 md:p-9">
          <p class="mb-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-accent">Zikr-i Batın</p>
          <h2 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-maintext">The inward dimension</h2>
          <div class="space-y-5 leading-8 text-subtext">
            <p>
              Zikr-i Batın became one of the albums closest to Köksal's personal
              musical taste. Works such as <em>Rahman Rahim Fert</em> and
              <em>Ya Mübdi, Ya Muid, Ya Muhyi, Ya Mumit</em> hold a particularly
              important place for him.
            </p>
            <p>
              The nineteenth work is built around the QUR'AN's Huruf al-Muqatta'at —
              letter sequences such as Alif Lam Mim — used directly as vocal musical
              material. Köksal regards this as one of the catalogue's most unusual
              experiments.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="pro-card p-7 md:p-9">
          <p class="mb-4 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-accent">ZIKR JAZZ</p>
          <h2 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-maintext">Extended harmony, the same center</h2>
          <div class="space-y-5 leading-8 text-subtext">
            <p>
              Jazz represented another missing language inside the growing universe.
              ZIKR JAZZ uses extended harmony — including sixths, sevenths, ninths and
              other expanded chord colors — together with modal thinking and the
              instrumental vocabulary associated with jazz.
            </p>
            <p>
              The result is intentionally warmer, calmer and more relaxed than the
              rock and metal albums, while remaining centered on the same act of
              remembrance.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </section>

      <!-- XXIII -->
      <section class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-[2rem] border border-accent/20 bg-accent/5 p-8 md:p-14">
        <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-accent">XXIII · ZIKR SYMPHONIC METAL</p>
        <h2 class="mb-8 text-4xl font-heading font-bold leading-tight text-maintext md:text-6xl">
          Nineteen movements. One sacred utterance.
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-8 text-subtext">
          <p>
            On 23 August 2026, Köksal submitted
            <strong class="text-maintext">ZIKR SYMPHONIC METAL</strong> for digital
            distribution. He regards it as one of the projects that most completely
            realizes the concept-album sound he had wanted to create for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The album is built around 19 movements and an unusually strict vocal
            concept. Almost the entire cycle is centered on one sacred utterance:
            <strong class="text-maintext">HÛ</strong>. In selected passages,
            <strong class="text-maintext">Hay / Al-Hayy</strong> also appears. Long
            lyrical texts are removed; instead, the same utterance passes through
            changing musical states.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breath and intimate female voice expand into sacred choir, strings, pipe
            organ, brass, timpani, bass, drums, distorted guitars and neoclassical
            solos. Metal is not treated as anger. In Köksal's conception, its weight
            represents power, scale and divine majesty.
          </p>

          <p>
            The orchestra is not background decoration, the choir is not an effect
            and the guitar is not included merely to display virtuosity. Each element
            serves the same center: zikr.
          </p>

          <p class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold leading-9 text-maintext md:text-3xl">
            The utterance does not change. The world of sound around it does.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- XXIV -->
      <section class="pro-glass p-8 md:p-14">
        <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-accent">XXIV · What Neo-Sacred Zikr Means</p>
        <h2 class="mb-8 text-4xl font-heading font-bold leading-tight text-maintext md:text-5xl">
          A musical universe intended to outlive its creator
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-8 text-subtext">
          <p>
            For Berk Köksal, Neo-Sacred Zikr is not a single genre. It is an upper
            musical definition: a contemporary, monotheistic sacred music universe in
            which different genres can be used to remember and glorify the ONE GOD.
          </p>

          <p>
            A Neo-Sacred Zikr work may be rock, metal, classical, ambient, jazz, rap,
            children's choir, piano-centered music, progressive music or a form that
            has not yet been created. The sound is variable. The center is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Köksal does not want the term to remain merely a private label attached to
            his own catalogue. His hope is that future monotheist musicians may use,
            develop and reinterpret the idea as a genuine field of music.
          </p>

          <p>
            He also accepts that the work may not be fully understood in its own time.
            Social-media numbers and immediate popularity are not the primary measure
            by which he evaluates the project. He has often expressed the belief that
            the musical idea may be understood more clearly decades — perhaps even a
            century — in the future.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- XXV -->
      <section class="grid grid-cols-1 items-center gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-16">
        <div class="biography-image lg:order-2">
          <img
            src="/artist/biography/berk-koksal-family.webp"
